\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
%
% TRANSFORMATION TNegAuxAdapt
< m1: SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}
              [ mu1,
                I1:auxrel/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu2],
                negrel/NEG(KEY_notNEGkey){NEGrec1},
                mu3
              ]
>

< m : SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}
              [ mu1,
                auxrel/VERB{VERBrec2}
                      [ I1:head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu2],
                        gluerel/GLUE{GLUErec1},
                        negrel/NEG(KEY_notNEGkey){NEGrec1}
                      ],
                mu3
              ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: VERBrec1.subc IN [modalverb, beverb, doaux, haveverb]
           AND 
           NOT EXIST (mu2, [head/SUBVERB{SUBVERBrec1}
                                [I11:head/BVERB(KEY1){BVERBrec1}] ])
         I11: KEY1 = KEY_may1BVERBkey OR
              Key1 = KEY_may2BVERBkey
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: VERBrec1 <> { subc    : beVerb
                            persons : [1]
                            numbers : [singular] 
                            tense   : presenttense }
          A1: VERBrec2 := VERBrec1;
              VERBrec2.subc := notaux
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: VERBrec2 = VERBrec1 EXCEPT FOR [subc] AND
              VERBrec2.subc = notaux AND
              ( VERBrec1 <> { subc    : beVerb
                              persons : [1]
                              numbers : [singular] 
                              tense   : presenttense } OR
                SENTENCErec1 = { mood      IN [yesnointerrogative,
                                               whinterrogative]
                                 senttype   : mainclause
                                 modus      : indicative
                                 finiteness : finite } )
          A1: @
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TNegAuxAdapt
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}
He did not do it $\rightarrow$ He didn't do it\\
They are not at home $\rightarrow$ They aren't at home\\
You must not do that $\rightarrow$ You mustn't do that\\
He can not sing $\rightarrow$ he can't sing etc.

In analysis also: \\
I am not in the right mood $\rightarrow$ I aren't in the right mood (although 
only needed in tag questions: I am smothering you, aren't I?) (see 
modifications 11/12/89)\\
I am not in the right mood $\rightarrow$ I ain't in the right mood

A different glue rule exists for {\em cannot\/}, using a 
different string than `not', i.e.\ `nòt'. The rule is ordered only after the 
inversion rules, since a 
construction like {\em Cannot you be quiet\/} seems awkward, while {\em He 
cannot come\/} is perfectly OK. 
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] ClauseToSentence
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_NegAuxAdapt
\item[* date of creation:] 9-FEB-1989 14:00:20  
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:]
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 24/10/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Excluded {\em may\/} from the rule, since in generation the system crashed on 
it (there is no glue rule for this case). The skey `maymightBVERBkey' has not 
been excluded, since there is a rule for {\em mightn't\/}. I do not know 
whether this same key is also used for a present tense; in that case, thre 
still can be crashes.
\item date: 11/12/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Added condition on present tense of {\em be\/}, since {\em I wasn't\/} is of 
course allowed;\\
Excluded {\em aren't/ain't\/} in analysis when the sentence is not a 
(presenttense) question. This should be limited even further, so that they are 
only accepted in tag questions, but these are not dealt with in Rosetta yet. 
Also, if {\em aren't\/} must ever be generated for tag questions, there is a 
problem with the glue rules: they now make both {\em aren't\/} and 
{\em ain't\/}  from {\em am\/} + GLUE + {\em not\/}. Perhaps here a second {\em 
n\`{o}t\/} must be used too, just like for {\em cannot\/} (see 
TC\_CanNegIncorp); if that is done, the current rule must have an extra subrule 
to deal with that {\em n\`{o}t\/} in analysis.
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to contract the negation and the modal or auxiliary 
(prior to inversion = TC\_AuxToComp, in case of interrogatives). 
The rule must be ordered after DoBeDeletion, since 
otherwise {\em be\/} and {\em not\/} would not be adjacent yet.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) It has not been 
decided yet whether the transformation should be optional, or should work in 
analysis only. A filter FPreNegAuxAdapt has been written to ensure that no
incorporated NEGs remain in analysis.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
