\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% TRANSFORMATION TAuxToComp
< m1: I5::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}
                  [ I1:sig1,
                    subjrel/T1,
                    I2:sig2,
                    auxrel/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu2],
                    I3:mu3
                  ]
>

< m : I5::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}
                  [ I1:sig1,
                    conjrel/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu2],
                    subjrel/T1,
                    I2:sig2,
                    I3:mu3
                  ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: ALL (sig1, shiftrel/..)
       I2: ALL (sig2, I21:rel1/..)
       I3: NOT EXIST (mu3, [I21:rel1/..])
         I21: rel1 IN AUX_preauxAdvRels
       I5: SENTENCErec1.senttype = mainclause
       m1: SENTENCErec1.mood = yesnointerrogative OR
           EXIST (sig1, [shiftrel/..]) 
       m : SENTENCErec1.mood = yesnointerrogative OR
           EXIST (sig1, [shiftrel/..]) 
      >


      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TAuxToComp
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}\\
1. Who you did see leave $\rightarrow$ Who did you see leave\\
2. In which garden/Where you did see that rose $\rightarrow$ In which garden/
where did you see that rose\\
3. You did leave $\rightarrow$ Did you leave\\
4. No sooner he had seen me, than ... $\rightarrow$ No sooner had he seen me 
than...\\
5. You did not leave $\rightarrow$ Did you not leave\\
6. You didn't leave $\rightarrow$ Didn't you leave (the `notaux' is moved; this 
verb has the original verb and the negation under it)
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] clauseTOsentence
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_AuxToComp
\item[* date of creation:] 24-MAY-1988 11:12:20  
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:]
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] inversion of subject and finite for yes/no questions and for 
sentences with a shiftrel
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) In case of a 
shiftrel, it is assumed that we are always dealing with a wh-shift or an adverb 
or 
conjunction (how to know what should be placed in shiftrel and 
which one shouldn't is as yet unclear to me; in shiftrel are the
composite conjunction {\em No sooner ... than\/} and sentence-inital 
occurrences of the adverbs {\em hardly (... when), 
seldom, scarcely, rarely, little\/}, and {\em barely\/}).\\
For topicalisation causing inversion (always when a negative adjunct is 
preposed, except {\em not yet\/}), a shiftrel is assumed ({\em Not a finger 
did I lay on him, Never will I make that mistake again\/}), while topicalisation 
not causing inversion would bear the 
relation leftdislocrel ({\em ?This book she gave me\/}). Relative shift only 
occurs with T2.CAT = ..VAR, and hence is excluded from the present rule.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
Subject - operator inversion in other cases has not been accounted for yet
(Quirk, pp. 948 - 950):\\
1. So + ADV was ... that/as to ... (So dark it was that I couldn't find it)\\
\ \ \ (Same for `In such a/to such + NP ... that ...)\\
\ \ \ Perhaps emphatic fronting of all so/such elements causes inversion; a 
case of shiftrel topicalisation?\\
2. Preposed `So' as object or predicate substitute: So say the rest of us (Note
that this is inversion of the {\em verb\/}, not of the auxiliary!), So do we\\
3. Optative subjunctive: Far be it ..., Suffice it to ..., Long live ...; May .
.. (p.\ 411-412)\\
4. Only in this way ...; Often had I intended ...; Well do I remember\\
5. Throwing the hammer is champion William Anderson, who...\\
Subject - verb inversion (without any operator) is described by Quirk only for
ergatives (p. 948): Here comes the bus, There, at the summit, stood the castle,
Away went the car like a whirlwind etc.
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}
\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% TRANSFORMATION Tauxtocomp2
< m1: I5::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}
                  [ I1:sig1,
                    I2:sig2,
                    auxrel/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu2],
                    I4:sig4,
                    I3:mu3
                  ]
>

< m : I5::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}
                  [ I1:sig1,
                    conjrel/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu2],
                    I2:sig2,
                    I4:sig4,
                    I3:mu3
                  ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: ALL (sig1, shiftrel/..)
       I2: ALL (sig2, I21:rel1/..)
       I3: NOT EXIST (mu3, [I21:rel1/..])
         I21: rel1 IN AUX_preauxAdvRels
       I4: ALL (sig4, negrel/..)
       I5: SENTENCErec1.senttype = mainclause
       m1: SENTENCErec1.mood = imperativemood AND
           (EXIST (mu2, [negrel/..]) OR
            EXIST (sig4,[negrel/..]))
       m : SENTENCErec1.mood = imperativemood AND
           (EXIST (mu2, [negrel/..]) OR
            EXIST (sig4,[negrel/..]))
      >


      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
